From c4988dc45a0781582b34cfdeb1b2bfc98ce9d627 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HempFiend <17104973+jaidenscool1@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 16:20:06 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] vma: textures give way on 8 GB cards; buffers, render targets
 and sparse pages from a pageable priority pool

Marvel's Wolverine's first fight on an RTX 4060 8 GB (base mode, 1664x936) ran at 3-5 fps:
the room of a fifth of the budget (f6117511) kept 1.4 GiB free for sparse buffer pages and sent
2.4 GB to system memory instead, among it a 248 MiB buffer a compute pass reads and writes every
frame (PCIe 1.2 GB/s in, 1.8-2.8 GB/s out; 150-180 ms of GPU work per frame). A small room ran
fast but exited on the next sparse page (NVIDIA allows sparse buffers video memory only), and the
texture cache cannot free textures for it: the game samples them through its image heap.

On cards under 10 GiB now:
- VK_EXT_pageable_device_local_memory + VK_EXT_memory_priority (when the device has them, on
  every card): a full card pages memory out instead of failing an allocation.
- Buffers, render targets/depth and sparse buffer pages come from one VMA pool at priority 1.0
  and ignore the room; textures stop going to video memory 1 GiB before the budget and stay in
  the default blocks (priority 0.5), which the driver pages first. Larger cards keep 512 MiB.

Fight: 15-16 fps (x4 runs, >= 3 min each, no sparse failures), 43 ms of GPU work per frame.
Debug: KYTY_NO_PAGEABLE=1, KYTY_VRAM_ROOM_MB (1428 = the old room), KYTY_TEXTURE_ROOM_MB,
KYTY_VRAM_TRACE (buffers and images placed in system memory), SparseBufferBytes counter.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
